<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" id="top">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>{{ title }}</title>

    {# Everything below comes from a CDN, so every url is pinned to an exact
       version and carries the `integrity` hash of that version's bytes. A
       host that starts serving something else then serves it into a page
       holding the user's `nf_jwt`, and the browser refuses it instead. The
       hashes are of the pinned files themselves — recompute one with
       `curl -sL <url> | openssl dgst -sha384 -binary | openssl base64 -A`
       if you change a version, and check the page still draws: a wrong hash
       blocks the file silently, and the whole page is drawn by these. #}
    <!-- CSS -->
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/twitter-bootstrap/3.4.1/css/bootstrap.min.css" integrity="sha384-HSMxcRTRxnN+Bdg0JdbxYKrThecOKuH5zCYotlSAcp1+c8xmyTe9GYg1l9a69psu" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer">
    {# Font Awesome 6 only. It defines the `fa`, `fas` and `far` prefixes the
       markup uses, so the 4.7.0 stylesheet that used to sit alongside it was
       being overridden for every icon on the page. The one thing that did
       still come from 4.7.0 is the `FontAwesome` font family `main.css` names
       for the collapsible arrows; that now names this file's family. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.7.2/css/all.min.css" integrity="sha384-nRgPTkuX86pH8yjPJUAFuASXQSSl2/bBUiNV47vSYpKFxHJhbcrGnmlYpYJMeD7a" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer">

    <link rel="icon" href="/img/favicon.ico" type="image/x-icon"/>
    {# `_includes/css/main.css`, emitted at this url by `css/main.njk`. #126
       dropped a <link> here because nothing emitted the file and every page
       load spent a request on a 404; emitting it is the other half of that, so
       the <link> comes back and the stylesheet stops being re-sent inline with
       every HTML response. The name carries a digest of the file, which is
       what lets `netlify.toml` cache `/css/*` for a year: change the CSS and
       this url changes with it. #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ assets.css.url }}">

    {# The page cannot draw until every script below has been fetched and run,
       so anything not needed to draw it carries `defer` and gets out of the
       way. What is left is what the block of app code at the end of this head
       reaches for while it is being parsed: jQuery and the libraries the first
       render calls into.

       Three of these are gone rather than deferred. Ramda was 44,743 bytes
       for four calls — three `R.equals` and one `R.isNil` — and axios 65,099
       for the two in `utils/http.js`; `utils/deep_equal.js` and `fetch` are
       what do that work now, so 110 KB stopped blocking the first paint of
       every page on the site. The `ramda` in `package.json` is a different
       thing and stays: `src/api` imports it in four files and nothing here
       loads that copy.

       bootstrap-table was the third, at 145,282 bytes of js and css to draw
       every list on the site. What it actually supplied was the table markup,
       sort-on-header-click, the Columns dropdown, expand/collapse of a detail
       row, and a pagination nothing used; the search, the columns and every
       cell formatter were already ours. `js/utils/table_model.js` decides those
       four things and `js/utils/table_view.js` draws them. It is also what
       unblocks the next two steps. bootstrap-table is a jQuery plugin and has
       no jQuery-free build at any version it has ever published, so it was the
       one thing here that jQuery could not have been removed from underneath;
       and it was the only caller `bootstrap.min.js` had, for the Columns
       dropdown. The site's own 113 jQuery calls are step 5. #269. #}
    <!-- JavaScript -->
    {# DOMPurify is the only thing between another user's markdown and this
       page's DOM, and this page holds their reader's `nf_jwt` — so it is the
       one version here that is worth keeping at the newest clean release
       rather than the newest convenient one. 2.3.5 was four years and a major
       behind, with a critical prototype pollution (GHSA-p3vf-v8qc-cwcr, fixed
       in 2.4.2) and a nesting mXSS (GHSA-gx9m-whjm-85jf, fixed in 2.5.0)
       against it. Every 3.x below 3.4.13 is reported against too, which is
       why this lands on the newest rather than somewhere comfortable.
       The call is `DOMPurify.sanitize(html)` with no configuration, in
       `components/common.js` and `components/profile/biography.js`, and that
       signature is unchanged across the major — 3.0 dropped IE, which is the
       whole of what it broke. #}
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/dompurify/3.4.14/purify.min.js" integrity="sha384-46dPGH1XlTmj7bc50bqLjTdORXs/3EP2QpA/6EWbelYWOY9VGp+87RT61S3Mcslb" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></script>
    {# The entry form's two date fields, drawn long after the page is. The
       url used to name no version at all, so the code the site ran changed
       whenever the package published; this is the version that resolved to.

       ApexCharts was here on the same reasoning and is not any more. It is
       862,713 bytes — more than every render-blocking file above it put
       together — for four histograms on `/profile/:name`, and this layout is
       every page: `_redirects` rewrites every url to this one document, so
       every list and every entry form downloaded all of it to draw nothing
       with it. `defer` keeps a file from blocking the parser and does nothing
       whatever about fetching it. The profile asks for it when it mounts now,
       and `js/utils/load_script.js` is where the url, the hash and the reason
       live. #269. #}
    <script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/litepicker@2.0.12/dist/litepicker.js" integrity="sha384-TwHJTusQtbcoEHJcrF5aeo0MHBgj8lt8onFRrG7wKk8Q/LG9EphkEsqNRiVWzIP9" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></script>
    {# 1.11.1 was from 2014. 1.12.2 is the one release on the whole 1.x
       line where CVE-2015-9251 is fixed — a cross-origin ajax response typed
       `text/javascript` went to `jQuery.globalEval` whatever the request had
       asked for. The fix regressed in 1.12.3 and was not made again until
       3.0, which the advisory range states out loud as
       `<1.12.2 || >=1.12.3 <3.0.0`: the rest of the 1.x line is no better
       than 1.11.1 was, and 1.12.4 is strictly worse.

       It is a swap rather than a clearance. 1.12.0 is where CVE-2020-11022
       begins, so the count stays at three. What the swap buys beyond the
       trade is that jQuery's own documented workaround for 11022 and 11023 —
       reassigning `jQuery.htmlPrefilter` — cannot be applied at all before
       1.12. Clearing those two outright needs 3.5.0, and 1.x to 3.x is not a
       drop-in on a page that leans on jQuery this heavily. #254. #}
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/jquery/1.12.2/jquery.min.js" integrity="sha384-o6l2EXLcx4A+q7ls2O2OP2Lb2W7iBgOsYvuuRI6G+Efbjbk6J4xbirJpHZZoHbfs" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></script>
    {# 3.3.1 carried seven moderate XSS and 3.4.1 clears six of them. What
       is left is CVE-2024-6485, `data-loading-text` in the button plugin, and
       CVE-2025-1647, popover and tooltip, reported against 3.4.1 exactly.
       Neither has a patched release anywhere on the 3.x line, so they are the
       floor for as long as this page is on Bootstrap 3 — both need markup
       this site writes itself to carry an attacker's `data-*`. Bootstrap 3 to
       5 renames every class in every template and is its own change. #254. #}
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/twitter-bootstrap/3.4.1/js/bootstrap.min.js" integrity="sha384-aJ21OjlMXNL5UyIl/XNwTMqvzeRMZH2w8c5cRVpzpU8Y5bApTppSuUkhZXN0VxHd" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></script>
    {# 4.0.2 carried two high ReDoS advisories, both fixed in 4.0.10 — which
       cdnjs lists but has no files for, so the 4.x line here stops at 4.3.0
       and the choice was that or the current major. This is the current one.
       `marked.parse(md)` is the entire API this site uses and it is unchanged;
       what 9.0 removed is `headerIds` and `mangle`, neither of them passed
       here, so a note's headings no longer carry an `id` nobody linked to and
       an email address in a biography is no longer entity-mangled. The file
       moved to `lib/marked.umd.min.js` in 5.0; the plain `marked.min.js` name
       is gone, not merely a redirect, so the path is part of this bump. #}
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/marked/18.0.10/lib/marked.umd.min.js" integrity="sha384-2sZoZkY0dSopyVhL5X3bLyTfz4k3kFJVK7yoEHdbYOlt85O2vHQTT2uVOPxIlsmb" crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></script>

    {# The frontend's own forty files, concatenated and minified into one file
       by `_data/assets.js` — which is where the list and, more to the point,
       the order live. Inlining them here meant re-sending 140 KB with every
       HTML response.

       The url carries a digest of those bytes, so it changes exactly when they
       do. That is what `netlify.toml` is relying on when it serves `/js/*`
       with `immutable` for a year: the browser never has to ask whether this
       file is still current, because a new bundle arrives under a new name.

       `defer`, so fetching it does not block the parser — which matters on a
       page whose entire body is drawn by it. What used to prevent that was an
       inline block at the end of <body> reaching for `Components`: an inline
       script runs before a deferred one, so the bundle was not there yet. That
       block is now `js/boot.js`, the last file in the bundle, which puts it
       back after everything it depends on.

       The CDN scripts above are what the bundle reaches for while it runs.
       They stay ahead of it either way: the ones without `defer` run as the
       parser reaches them, and the one with it is earlier in the document, so
       it runs first. #}
    <script defer src="{{ assets.js.url }}"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    {#
      Every page is drawn client-side, so anything that fetches a url here and
      reads the HTML — a language model, a scraper, `curl` — finds an empty
      `#site` and concludes the list is empty. This is the one piece of the
      page that is in the source, and it says where the data actually is.
    #}
    <noscript>
      <h1>Memo</h1>
      <p>
        This page is rendered in the browser, so there is nothing to read in
        its source. The same lists are served as data, and that is what to
        fetch instead:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>/api/export/&lt;type&gt;/&lt;username&gt;</code> — one list, where <code>&lt;type&gt;</code> is <code>films</code>, <code>tv</code>, <code>games</code> or <code>books</code></li>
        <li><code>/api/export/&lt;username&gt;</code> — all four at once, if they fit in one response</li>
        <li>append <code>?format=md</code> for Markdown instead of JSON, or <code>?limit=200</code> for the most recently updated entries only</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both include every entry with its metadata, its score and its notes.
        For example, <a href="/api/export/films/nil">/api/export/films/nil</a>
        is what <a href="/films/nil">/films/nil</a> draws.
      </p>
    </noscript>
    <div id="site"></div>
  </body>
</html>



